      "title": "Determining Whether Three Vectors are Coplanar"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "622c95b8-758c-5e2d-bb54-c90f57c685b3",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=\u27e81,3,1\u27e9$, $b=\u27e8-3,0,-5\u27e9$, and $c=\u27e8-8,k,k\u27e9$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f37f6ea4-f6a6-5ab3-b3c7-bdc48c8715f9",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "3174fa04-6979-5998-8d00-d285b1fa0120",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "-\\frac{120}{11}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "3174fa04-6979-5998-8d00-d285b1fa0120"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -8 & k & k\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ k & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ -8 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ -8 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (k)-(-5)\\cdot (k)))+((-3)\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (k)-(-5)\\cdot (-8)))+((1)\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (-8)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (5k))+((-3)\\cdot (-3k-40))+((1)\\cdot (-3k))\\\\ &=(5k)+(9k+120)+(-3k)\\\\ &=11k+120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11k+120&=0\\\\ 11k&=-120\\\\ k&=-\\frac{120}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{120}{11}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
