      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & k^{2} \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-5)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-8 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((k^{2})\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-3)-(1)\\cdot (-2)))+((-5)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (-3)-(1)\\cdot (5)))+((k^{2})\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (5)))\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot (2))+((-5)\\cdot (19))+((k^{2})\\cdot (16))\\\\ &=(-6)+(-95)+(16k^{2})\\\\ &=16k^{2}-101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16k^{2}-101&=0\\\\ 16k^{2}&=101\\\\ k^{2}&=\\frac{101}{16}\\\\ k&=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{101}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{\\sqrt{101}}{4}$ or $k=\\frac{\\sqrt{101}}{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
