          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "8fca529c-d1d5-525b-b872-db33fe3fb7a5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & k & -6 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -8\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -6 \\\\ 1 & -8\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}|)+((-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-8)-(-6)\\cdot (1)))+((2)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-8)-(-6)\\cdot (-4)))+((-3)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (1)-(k)\\cdot (-4)))\\\\ &=((-3)\\cdot (6-8k))+((2)\\cdot (-8))+((-3)\\cdot (4k-2))\\\\ &=(24k-18)+(-16)+(6-12k)\\\\ &=12k-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12k-28&=0\\\\ 12k&=28\\\\ k&=\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{7}{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
