      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -7 \\\\ k & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 \\\\ k & -5\\end{aligned}|)+((7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ k & 5\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-5)-(-7)\\cdot (5)))+((-1)\\cdot ((-7)\\cdot (-5)-(-7)\\cdot (k)))+((7)\\cdot ((-7)\\cdot (5)-(0)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (35))+((-1)\\cdot (7k+35))+((7)\\cdot (-35))\\\\ &=(-70)+(-7k-35)+(-245)\\\\ &=-7k-350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7k-350&=0\\\\ -7k&=350\\\\ k&=-50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-50.$",
