      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & k^{2} \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & k^{2} \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & k^{2} \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((4)\\cdot (-2)-(k^{2})\\cdot (-2)))+((2)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(k^{2})\\cdot (-1)))+((-2)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(4)\\cdot (-1)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (2k^{2}-8))+((2)\\cdot (k^{2}-4))+((-2)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(4k^{2}-16)+(2k^{2}-8)+(0)\\\\ &=6k^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k^{2}-24&=0\\\\ 6k^{2}&=24\\\\ k^{2}&=4\\\\ k&=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-2$ or $k=2.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
