      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ k^{2} & -8 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-8 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k^{2} & -7 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k^{2} & -8 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (8)-(-7)\\cdot (2)))+((-1)\\cdot ((k^{2})\\cdot (8)-(-7)\\cdot (-5)))+((-1)\\cdot ((k^{2})\\cdot (2)-(-8)\\cdot (-5)))\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot (-50))+((-1)\\cdot (8k^{2}-35))+((-1)\\cdot (2k^{2}-40))\\\\ &=(200)+(35-8k^{2})+(40-2k^{2})\\\\ &=275-10k^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}275-10k^{2}&=0\\\\ -10k^{2}&=-275\\\\ k^{2}&=\\frac{55}{2}\\\\ k&=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{110}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{\\sqrt{110}}{2}$ or $k=\\frac{\\sqrt{110}}{2}.$",
