      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 7 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0 & k\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-5)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((7)\\cdot (k)-(-7)\\cdot (0)))+((-5)\\cdot ((8)\\cdot (k)-(-7)\\cdot (5)))+((1)\\cdot ((8)\\cdot (0)-(7)\\cdot (5)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (7k))+((-5)\\cdot (8k+35))+((1)\\cdot (-35))\\\\ &=(7k)+(-40k-175)+(-35)\\\\ &=-33k-210\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-33k-210&=0\\\\ -33k&=210\\\\ k&=-\\frac{70}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{70}{11}.$",
