      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-8 & -1 & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-8)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (2)-(-6)\\cdot (1)))+((1)\\cdot ((4)\\cdot (2)-(-6)\\cdot (5)))+((k)\\cdot ((4)\\cdot (1)-(3)\\cdot (5)))\\\\ &=((-8)\\cdot (12))+((1)\\cdot (38))+((k)\\cdot (-11))\\\\ &=(-96)+(38)+(-11k)\\\\ &=-11k-58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11k-58&=0\\\\ -11k&=58\\\\ k&=-\\frac{58}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{58}{11}.$",
