      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -7 & k & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & k\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (k)))+((-2)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (-7)))+((-4)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (k)-(5)\\cdot (-7)))\\\\ &=((-4)\\cdot (0))+((-2)\\cdot (0))+((-4)\\cdot (k+35))\\\\ &=(0)+(0)+(-4k-140)\\\\ &=-4k-140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4k-140&=0\\\\ -4k&=140\\\\ k&=-35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-35.$",
