      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & k & k \\\\ -7 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((5)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & k \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((5)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ -7 & 3\\end{aligned}|)+((-6)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ -7 & -3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((5)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (3)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((5)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (3)-(k)\\cdot (-7)))+((-6)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-3)-(k)\\cdot (-7)))\\\\ &=((5)\\cdot (6k))+((5)\\cdot (7k+9))+((-6)\\cdot (7k-9))\\\\ &=(30k)+(35k+45)+(54-42k)\\\\ &=23k+99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23k+99&=0\\\\ 23k&=-99\\\\ k&=-\\frac{99}{23}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{99}{23}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
