          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "9f6b9d79-1c96-5e9d-aa4b-5983d6a4c981"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}4 & k^{2} & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-k^{2})\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((4)\\cdot ((4)\\cdot (-3)-(-8)\\cdot (3)))+((-k^{2})\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-8)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (3)-(4)\\cdot (0)))\\\\ &=((4)\\cdot (12))+((-k^{2})\\cdot (3))+((-2)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(48)+(-3k^{2})+(6)\\\\ &=54-3k^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54-3k^{2}&=0\\\\ -3k^{2}&=-54\\\\ k^{2}&=18\\\\ k&=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-3\\sqrt{2}$ or $k=3\\sqrt{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
